Q: Is it possible in C (not invoking UB) to check if two objects overlap? A: <p>Well, if we're going to be <a href="/questions/tagged/language-lawyer" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;language-lawyer&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;language-lawyer&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-language-lawyer-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">language-lawyer</a>ing, I raise you this:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// SPDX-License-Identifier: Unlicense

#include &lt;stddef.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

bool overlap(const void *p1, size_t s1, const void *p2, size_t s2)
{
    const uintptr_t p1b = (uintptr_t) p1;
    const uintptr_t p2b = (uintptr_t) p2;
    const uintptr_t p1e = (uintptr_t) ((char*) p1 + s1);
    const uintptr_t p2e = (uintptr_t) ((char*) p2 + s2);
    return (p1b &lt;= p2b &amp;&amp; p2b &lt; p1e)
        || (p2b &lt;= p1b &amp;&amp; p1b &lt; p2e);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This code invokes <em>implementation-defined</em> behavior, not <em>undefined</em> behavior.[1] Obviously, this is by no means portable, but in most cases this should work.</p>
<hr />
<p>[1]: ISO/IEC 9899:2018, § 6.3.2.3, par. 6 (&quot;Any pointer type may be converted to an integer type. Except as previously specified, the result
is implementation-defined.&quot;).</p>
